**INTERCEPTA**

Complete Disease Net Architecture

mCRPC Phase 1 --- Corrected and Research-Verified

Version 2.0 \| March 12, 2026 \| CONFIDENTIAL

Every data source, dataset accession, cell count, API endpoint, and
mutation frequency in this document has been verified against published
peer-reviewed literature and public database records as of March 2026.
Nothing is assumed. Nothing is hand-picked. Where data is uncertain, it
is marked as such.

**Part 1: Why the Previous Approach Was Wrong**

Our initial Phase 1 work attempted to validate the two-population ODE by
manually choosing Emax, EC50, growth rates, and initial tumor fractions,
then adjusting these parameters until the simulated hazard ratio matched
clinical trial targets. This is curve fitting --- the chain approach our
vision was designed to replace. Every parameter in the ODE must trace to
measured data through a documented scientific pipeline. If the predicted
HR does not match the clinical trial, that is a scientific finding
telling us something is missing from the net. It is not a signal to
tweak numbers.

The root cause: our disease net had 37 nodes and 25 hand-picked edges. A
real mCRPC net must contain every relevant biological entity and every
measured relationship from public databases. The SU2C/PCF mCRPC study
alone profiled 429 patients (Abida et al., PNAS 2019, cBioPortal ID:
prad_su2c_2019) with whole-exome and transcriptome sequencing ---
identifying hundreds of recurrent alterations. We used 12 genes. That
gap is why our simulation failed.

**Part 2: The 10-Layer Net --- Research-Verified Specification**

Each layer below specifies exactly what data it contains, the specific
public database and accession number that provides it, the programmatic
access method verified to work, and the expected node/edge count for
mCRPC based on published cohort sizes.

**Layer 1: Genomic --- Mutations, Copy Numbers, Structural Variants**

**What it captures:** Every somatic mutation, copy number alteration,
gene fusion, and structural variant observed in mCRPC tumors. Driver vs
passenger classification. Germline pathogenic variants (BRCA2, ATM,
BRCA1). Mutation co-occurrence and mutual exclusivity patterns. Mutation
enrichment in mCRPC relative to primary prostate cancer.

**Published mutation frequencies (SU2C/PCF, Robinson et al. 2015, Abida
et al. 2019):** AR alterations 40-60%, TP53 40-60%, PTEN 40-60%, ETS
fusions 40-60%, RB1 \~21%, BRCA2 \~13%, BRCA1 \~6%, ATM \~8%, SPOP
\~11%, FOXA1 \~10%, PIK3CA \~5%, CDK12 \~7%, MYC amplification \~12%.
DNA repair pathway aberrations overall: 19.3% (Robinson et al. 2015).
89% of mCRPC patients harbor at least one clinically actionable
alteration.

**Intrinsic molecular subtypes (Hamid et al., Cancer Res 2023):**
Analysis of 634 mCRPC samples identified four subtypes: adeno-classic
(largest), adeno-immune (higher immune pathway expression), NE-classic
(neuroendocrine), NE-liver (liver metastasis tropism, PTEN loss). Both
NE subtypes have worse survival than adenocarcinoma subtypes.

